There is some demand - for example the tis package has had nberShade.ggplot (adds recessions to ggplot2 graphs) and fortify.tis (create a data frame from tis time series objects) for a couple of years.  <br><br>You should probably rename as.zoo.df to fortify.zoo since ``fortify`` is the standard function for creating data frames for future plotting with ggplot2.<br>
<br>I also like some kind of autoplot construct like this.  <br><br>autoplot.zoo &lt;- function(x, ...) { <br>    if(is.null(ncol(x)) == 1) {<br>        qplot(as.Date(time(x)), coredata(x), geom=&quot;line&quot;) + xlab(&quot;&quot;) + ylab(substitute(x))<br>
    } else if(ncol(x) == 1) {<br>        qplot(as.Date(time(x)), as.vector(coredata(x)), geom=&quot;line&quot;) + xlab(&quot;&quot;) + ylab(substitute(x))<br>    } else {<br>        df &lt;- data.frame(time=as.Date(time(x)), coredata(x))<br>
        dfm &lt;- reshape2::melt(df, id.vars=&quot;time&quot;, <a href="http://variable.name" target="_blank" rel="nofollow" data-saferedirecturl="https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://variable.name&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792374601051000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3KQFHAotHR1W6qy1i-UxCH">variable.name</a>=&quot;series&quot;)<br>        qplot(time, value, data=dfm, geom=&quot;line&quot;, group=series, colour=series, linetype=series)<br>
    }<br>}<br><br><br><div class="gmail_quote"></div>
